.TH SED 1 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sed \- stream editor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sed
.RB [ \-EnrsSz ]
.RB [ \-i \fR[\fIext\fR]]
.RB [ \-l
.IR width ]
.I script
.RI [ file ...]
.br
.B sed
.RB [ \-EnrsSz ]
.RB [ \-i \fR[\fIext\fR]]
.RB [ \-l
.IR width ]
.RB [ \-e
.IR script ]...
.RB [ \-f
.IR script_file ]...
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sed
reads each
.I file
in turn (standard input if none is given; a
.I file
of
.B \-
also means standard input), applies the editing
.I script
to every input line, and writes the result to standard output.
.PP
Each cycle reads one line into the
.I pattern space
(without its newline), runs the script from the top, and then, unless
.B \-n
is in effect or the cycle was ended by a command that suppresses it,
writes the pattern space followed by a newline.
Text queued by
.BR a ,
.B r
and
.B R
is written after that.
A second buffer, the
.IR "hold space" ,
starts empty and keeps its contents between cycles.
.PP
Without
.BR \-s ,
line numbers continue from one input file to the next.
The regular expression engine is the Substrate
.BR regex (3)
library: basic regular expressions by default, extended ones with
.BR \-E .
Comparisons are byte-based.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be combined in one argument
.RB ( \-ns );
option parsing stops at the first argument not beginning with
.BR \- ,
at a lone
.BR \- ,
or after
.BR \-\- .
If no
.B \-e
or
.B \-f
is given, the first operand is the script.
.TP
.BR \-E ", " \-r
Use extended regular expressions.
.TP
.BI \-e " script"
Append
.I script
and a newline to the script.
The argument may also be attached
.RB ( \-e\fIscript\fR ).
.TP
.BI \-f " script_file"
Append the contents of
.I script_file
and a newline to the script.
.TP
.BR \-i [\fIext\fR]
Edit each
.I file
in place.
Output for each file is written to a temporary file next to it, which
receives the original's mode and owner, is synced to disk, and is then
renamed over the original.
If
.I ext
is given, the original is first renamed to its name with
.I ext
appended.
.I ext
must be attached: all characters after
.B i
in the same argument are taken as the extension, so
.B \-ni
means
.B \-n \-i
but
.B \-in
means
.B \-i
with extension
.BR n .
If writing the temporary file fails, the original is left unchanged.
Files are never read from standard input in this mode.
.TP
.BI \-l " width"
Set the default line-wrap width for the
.B l
command (default 70).
.TP
.B \-n
Do not write the pattern space at the end of each cycle.
A script whose first two characters are
.B #n
has the same effect.
.TP
.B \-s
Treat files as separate: line numbers and range states are reset at the
start of each file.
.TP
.B \-S
Sandbox: the
.BR r ,
.BR R ,
.BR w ,
.BR W ,
and
.B e
commands and the
.B w
flag of
.B s
do nothing.
No output file is created or truncated.
Use this for scripts that are not trusted, since otherwise a script can
read and write any file the user can, and run shell commands.
.TP
.B \-z
Input lines are separated by NUL bytes instead of newlines (but see
.BR BUGS ).
.SH SCRIPT SYNTAX
Commands are separated by newlines or semicolons; blanks before a
command are ignored.
A line beginning with
.B #
is a comment.
.PP
Each command may be preceded by zero, one or two addresses and by
.B !
to invert the selection.
.SS Addresses
.TP
.I n
Line number
.I n
(cumulative across files unless
.BR \-s ).
.TP
.B $
The last line of the current input file.
.TP
.BI / re /
Lines matching the regular expression
.IR re .
.BI \e c re c
uses any character
.I c
as delimiter.
Within
.IR re ,
.B \en
matches a newline and
.BI \e c
a literal delimiter.
A trailing
.B I
makes the match case-insensitive.
An empty
.I re
reuses the previous regular expression in the script text.
.TP
.IB first ~ step
Every
.IR step th
line starting at line
.IR first .
.TP
.IB addr1 , addr2
A range from a line matching
.I addr1
through the next line matching
.IR addr2 .
If
.I addr2
is a line number not greater than the line that matched
.IR addr1 ,
only that one line is selected.
A regular-expression
.I addr2
is first tried on the line after
.IR addr1 .
.TP
.BI 0, / re /
Like
.BI 1, / re /
but
.I re
may already end the range on line 1.
.TP
.IB addr1 ,+ N
.I addr1
and the
.I N
lines after it.
.TP
.IB addr1 ,~ N
From
.I addr1
up to the next line whose number is a multiple of
.IR N .
.SS Commands
.TP
.BR { " ... " }
Group commands under one address.
Braces nest up to 64 deep.
.TP
.BR a " \fItext\fR, " i " \fItext\fR, " c " \fItext\fR"
Append
.I text
after the cycle's output, insert it immediately, or delete the pattern
space and output
.I text
instead
.RB ( c
on a range writes
.I text
once, at the end of the range).
.I text
may follow the command on the same line, or follow
.B \e
and a newline.
Lines ending in a backslash continue the text;
.BR \en ,
.B \et
and
.B \e\e
within it are replaced by newline, tab and backslash.
.TP
.BI b " label"
Branch to
.IR label ,
or to the end of the script if
.I label
is omitted.
.TP
.BI : label
Define a label.
.TP
.B d
Delete the pattern space and start the next cycle.
.TP
.B D
Delete the pattern space up to and including the first newline and
restart the script on what remains without reading input.
.TP
.BR e " [\fIcommand\fR]"
Run
.I command
with
.BR system (3);
without
.IR command ,
run the pattern space as a command and then empty the pattern space.
The command's output goes straight to standard output.
.TP
.B F
Write the current file name: the operand as given, or
.B (stdin)
when no files were named.
.TP
.BR g ", " G
.B g
replaces the pattern space with the hold space;
.B G
appends a newline and the hold space to the pattern space.
.TP
.BR h ", " H
.B h
replaces the hold space with the pattern space;
.B H
appends a newline and the pattern space to the hold space.
.TP
.BR l " [\fIwidth\fR]"
Write the pattern space unambiguously: backslash escapes for
.BR \e\e ,
.BR \ea ,
.BR \eb ,
.BR \ef ,
.BR \en ,
.BR \er ,
.BR \et ,
.BR \ev ,
three-digit octal for other non-printable bytes, a
.B $
at the end, and lines broken with a trailing
.B \e
before
.I width
columns (default from
.BR \-l ;
a width of 1 disables wrapping).
.TP
.B n
Write the pattern space (unless
.BR \-n ),
then replace it with the next input line.
At the end of input,
.B sed
exits.
.TP
.B N
Append a newline and the next input line to the pattern space.
At the end of input, the pattern space is written (unless
.BR \-n )
and
.B sed
exits.
.TP
.BR p ", " P
Write the pattern space, or its first line.
.TP
.BR q " [\fIstatus\fR], " Q " [\fIstatus\fR]"
Quit, with exit status
.I status
(default 0).
.B q
first writes the pattern space (unless
.BR \-n )
and any queued text;
.B Q
writes only the queued text.
.TP
.BI r " file"
Queue the contents of
.I file
for output at the end of the cycle.
A missing file is ignored.
.TP
.BI R " file"
Queue the first line of
.IR file .
.TP
.BI s/ re / replacement / flags
Substitute
.I replacement
for matches of
.IR re .
Any character may replace
.BR / .
In
.IR replacement ,
.B &
is the whole match,
.BR \e1 " ... " \e9
are subexpressions,
.BR \en ,
.B \et
are newline and tab,
.B \e&
and
.B \e\e
are literal, and
.BR \eu ,
.B \el
convert the next character and
.BR \eU ,
.B \eL
the following text to upper or lower case until
.BR \eE .
.I flags
may be:
.B g
(replace all matches),
a single digit
.I N
(replace only the
.IR N th
match),
.B p
(write the pattern space if a substitution was made),
.B i
or
.B I
(case-insensitive),
.B e
(accepted but ignored), and
.BI w " file"
(append the pattern space to
.I file
if a substitution was made; must be the last flag).
An empty
.I re
reuses the previous regular expression in the script text.
.TP
.BI t " label"
Branch if a substitution has been made since the last input line was
read or the last
.B t
or
.B T
was taken.
.TP
.BI T " label"
Branch if no substitution has been made.
.TP
.BI w " file"
Append the pattern space to
.IR file .
.TP
.BI W " file"
Append the first line of the pattern space to
.IR file .
.TP
.B x
Exchange the pattern and hold spaces.
.TP
.BI y/ source / dest /
Replace each byte of
.I source
with the byte at the same position in
.IR dest .
Both must have the same length;
.BR \en ,
.BR \et ,
.BR \ea ,
.B \er
and backslash-escaped delimiters are recognised.
.TP
.B z
Empty the pattern space.
.TP
.B =
Write the current line number.
.PP
For
.BR r ,
.BR R ,
.BR w ,
.B W
and the
.B w
flag, one space after the command is skipped and the file name runs to
the end of the line or the next
.BR ; .
Output files are opened (and truncated) when they are first written,
and each distinct name is opened only once; at most 32 names may be
used.
.PP
To stop runaway branch loops, a single cycle may execute at most
100000000 commands; beyond that
.B sed
exits with an error.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
A usage or script error, an input file that could not be opened (the
other files are still processed), a failed in-place update, or an
output file that could not be opened.
.TP
.I status
The value given to
.B q
or
.BR Q .
.SH EXAMPLES
Replace every
.B foo
with
.BR bar :
.EX
$ sed \(aqs/foo/bar/g\(aq file
.EE
.PP
Print lines 10 to 20:
.EX
$ sed \-n 10,20p file
.EE
.PP
Delete comment lines in place, keeping a backup:
.EX
$ sed \-i.orig \(aq/\(ha#/d\(aq config
.EE
.PP
Join all lines with commas:
.EX
$ sed \-e :a \-e \(aq$!N; s/\en/,/; ta\(aq file
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR awk (1),
.BR ed (1),
.BR grep (1),
.BR tr (1),
.BR regex (3)
.SH STANDARDS
Based on POSIX.1-2024
.BR sed ,
with extensions from BSD
.RB ( Q ,
.BR R ,
.BR T ,
.BR W ,
.IR first ~ step ,
the
.B I
modifier,
.B q
exit status) and GNU
.RB ( e ,
.BR F ,
.BR z ,
.BR 0, / re /,
.BR ,+ N ,
.BR ,~ N ,
case conversion in replacements).
There are no long options.
.SH BUGS
.B $
matches the last line of every input file, not only of the last one,
even without
.BR \-s .
.PP
A
.BI 0, / re /
range is started again on every line after it ends, so in practice it
selects every line.
.PP
.B q
with status 0 only stops the current file; later files are still
processed.
.PP
.B D
on a pattern space that contains no newline never reads more input and
loops forever.
.PP
The
.IR N g
flag combination replaces only the
.IR N th
match, and the
.B e
flag of
.B s
does nothing.
.PP
A backslash followed by a newline in a replacement produces a literal
backslash and newline instead of a newline.
.PP
.B R
reads the first line of its file every time instead of successive
lines.
.PP
With
.BR \-z ,
the NUL terminator is kept in the pattern space and a newline is added
on output.
.PP
The output of
.B e
commands may appear before earlier
.B sed
output, because standard output is not flushed first.
.PP
.B \(ha
and
.B $
in regular expressions also match next to newlines inside the pattern
space.
.PP
Any first script line beginning with
.B #n
turns on
.BR \-n ,
not only a line consisting of exactly
.BR #n .
An empty regular expression reuses the previous one in the script text
rather than the last one used at run time.
.PP
.B \-l 0
selects the default width of 70 instead of disabling wrapping.
.PP
Without
.BR \-s ,
.B \-i
does not reset line numbers between files.
The temporary file for
.B \-i
is named
.IB file .sed pid
and is opened without exclusive creation.
